      "q": "Practice: mark a todo done",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: mark a todo done. Map the list. Flip done on that id. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `if (t.id !== id) return t` — not this one — keep it. `return { ...t, done: true }` — Update — tick it. A line you can point at: function markDone(id) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. One tick does not wipe the rest. The object keeps its id. You can send only the changed fields. UPDATE without WHERE updates every row. In JS, skip items whose id does not match. Check ownership on the server. Validate the new text; do not save empty titles.\n\nThe problem before\nPeople replaced the whole list, or ticked every row, because they had no id. PATCH without an id is a dangerous UPDATE without WHERE. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Map the list. Flip done on that id. UPDATE without WHERE updates every row. In JS, skip items whose id does not match. Check ownership on the server. Validate the new text; do not save empty titles.\n\nWhat this is\nMap the list. Flip done on that id. Update means change fields on the matching id only. map in JS: if id matches, spread the old object and overwrite done or text. PATCH /todos/:id. SQL UPDATE ... WHERE id = $1. Walk the snippet like this: `if (t.id !== id) return t` — not this one — keep it. `return { ...t, done: true }` — Update — tick it.\n\nWhat it solves\nOne tick does not wipe the rest. The object keeps its id. You can send only the changed fields. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Map the list. Flip done on that id. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA marksheet. You change Ada's maths marks. You do not rewrite the whole class register. The roll number stays the same. Only one cell changes. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. mark a todo done. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nMark done, edit title, PATCH, UPDATE. Toggle a flag. Rename a card. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: mark a todo done. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. One tick does not wipe the rest. The object keeps its id. You can send only the changed fields.\n\nWatch out\nUPDATE without WHERE updates every row. In JS, skip items whose id does not match. Check ownership on the server. Validate the new text; do not save empty titles. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview.",
      "code": "function markDone(id) {\n  todos = todos.map((t) => {\n    if (t.id !== id) return t;  // not this one — keep it\n    return { ...t, done: true };  // Update — tick it\n  });\n}"
