      "q": "Practice: keep todos after refresh",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: keep todos after refresh. JSON.stringify into localStorage. Parse on load. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `localStorage.setItem(\"todos\", JSON.stringify(list` — save as text. `const raw = localStorage.getItem(\"todos\") || \"[]\"` — empty if first visit. `return JSON.parse(raw` — text back into objects. A line you can point at: function saveTodos(list) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Drafts survive refresh. Sessions can be safer in cookies. Interviewers want the three compared: lifetime, who can read, does the server see it. XSS plus localStorage JWT is a stolen account. Do not store passwords. Cookies have size limits and CSRF issues.\n\nThe problem before\nRefresh wiped the todos. Or the login token sat in localStorage where any XSS could read it. People used one junk drawer for theme, draft, and session. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. JSON.stringify into localStorage. Parse on load. XSS plus localStorage JWT is a stolen account. Do not store passwords. Cookies have size limits and CSRF issues.\n\nWhat this is\nJSON.stringify into localStorage. Parse on load. localStorage stays until cleared. sessionStorage dies with the tab. Cookies can be sent to the server; httpOnly cookies cannot be read by JS. Pick the shelf that matches the job. Walk the snippet like this: `localStorage.setItem(\"todos\", JSON.stringify(list` — save as text. `const raw = localStorage.getItem(\"todos\") || \"[]\"` — empty if first visit. `return JSON.parse(raw` — text back into objects. Drafts survive refresh. Sessions can be safer in cookies. Interviewers want the three compared: lifetime, who can read, does the server see it.\n\nWhat it solves\nDrafts survive refresh. Sessions can be safer in cookies. Interviewers want the three compared: lifetime, who can read, does the server see it. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). JSON.stringify into localStorage. Parse on load. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA price book in the drawer overnight is localStorage. Chalk on one slate that wipes when the window closes is sessionStorage. A sealed chit the runner takes to the kitchen is an httpOnly cookie. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. keep todos after refresh.\n\nUses\nTheme, todos in a lab, wizard drafts, session ids. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: keep todos after refresh. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Drafts survive refresh. Sessions can be safer in cookies. Interviewers want the three compared: lifetime, who can read, does the server see it.\n\nWatch out\nXSS plus localStorage JWT is a stolen account. Do not store passwords. Cookies have size limits and CSRF issues. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. JSON.stringify into localStorage. Parse on load. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.",
