      "q": "Practice: POST a todo to an API",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: POST a todo to an API. JSON body + Bearer token. Always HTTPS in production. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `const res = await fetch(\"https:` — api.example.com/todos\", {. `method: \"POST\"` — Create on the server. `Authorization: \"Bearer \" + token` — login ticket. `body: JSON.stringify({ text }` — the new task. A line you can point at: async function createOnServer(text, token) { In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Guests bounce. Users cannot wipe admin data. You can say both words in an interview without mixing them.\n\nThe problem before\nAnyone who knew the URL could act as Ada. Passwords sat in plain text. The UI hid a button and called that security. A stolen token never expired. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. JSON body + Bearer token. Always HTTPS in production. Hiding a button is not security. Do not trust req.body.role. Do not put JWT in localStorage if you can use an httpOnly cookie. Never store raw passwords. HTTPS for every login.\n\nWhat this is\nJSON body + Bearer token. Always HTTPS in production. Authentication is who you are: email + password, then a token or cookie. Authorization is what you may do: role or owner check. Hash passwords. The server decides 401 (not logged in) vs 403 (logged in but not allowed). Walk the snippet like this: `const res = await fetch(\"https:` — api.example.com/todos\", {. `method: \"POST\"` — Create on the server. `Authorization: \"Bearer \" + token` — login ticket. `body: JSON.stringify({ text }` — the new task.\n\nWhat it solves\nGuests bounce. Users cannot wipe admin data. You can say both words in an interview without mixing them. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). JSON body + Bearer token. Always HTTPS in production. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA library. The card at the door is authentication — you are Ada. The rare-book stamp is authorization — only staff may take that shelf. Hiding the door is polite. The librarian still checks the stamp. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet.\n\nUses\nLogin, signup, JWT, sessions, admin routes, owner-only delete. Middleware that reads Authorization. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: POST a todo to an API. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Guests bounce. Users cannot wipe admin data. You can say both words in an interview without mixing them.\n\nWatch out\nHiding a button is not security. Do not trust req.body.role. Do not put JWT in localStorage if you can use an httpOnly cookie. Never store raw passwords. HTTPS for every login. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview.",
      "code": "async function createOnServer(text, token) {\n  const res = await fetch(\"https://api.example.com/todos\", {\n    method: \"POST\",  // Create on the server\n    headers: {\n      \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n      Authorization: \"Bearer \" + token  // login ticket\n    },\n    body: JSON.stringify({ text })  // the new task\n  });\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(\"create failed\");\n  return res.json();\n}"
